\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s54 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Protocol Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSColorPickingDefault protocol, together with the NSColorPickingCustom protocol, provides an interface for adding color pickers\'d0custom user interfaces for color selection\'d0to an application\'27s NSColorPanel. The NSColorPickingDefault protocol provides basic behavior for a color picker. The NSColorPickingCustom protocol provides implementation-specific behavior. \
\fs14\
\fs28 The NSColorPicker class implements the NSColorPickingDefault protocol. The simplest way to implement your own color picker is to create a subclass of NSColorPicker, implementing the NSColorPickingCustom protocol for that subclass. However, it\'27s possible to create a subclass of another class, such as NSView, and use it as a base upon which to add the methods of both NSColorPickingDefault and NSColorPickingCustom.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Color Picker Bundles\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 A class that implements the NSColorPickingDefault and NSColorPickingCustom protocols needs to be compiled and linked in an application\'27s object file. However, your application need not explicitly create an instance of this class. Instead, your application\'27s file package should include a directory named \b ColorPickers\b0 ; within this directory you should place a directory \i MyPickerClass\b \i0 .bundle\b0  for each custom color picker your application implements. This bundle should contain all resources required for your color picker: nib files, TIFF files, and so on. \
\fs14\
\fs28 NSColorPanel will allocate and initialize an instance of each class for which a bundle is found in the \b ColorPickers\b0  directory. The class name is assumed to be the bundle directory name minus the \b .bundle\b0  extension.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Color Picker Buttons\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSColorPanel lets the user select a color picker from an NSMatrix of NSButtonCells. This protocol includes methods for providing and manipulating the image that gets displayed on the button.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Color Mask and Color Modes\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The color mask determines which color mode is enabled for NSColorPanel. This mask is set before you initialize a new instance of NSColorPanel. NSColorPanelAllModesMask represents the logical OR of the other color mask constants: It causes the NSColorPanel to display all standard color pickers. When initializing a new instance of NSColorPanel, you can logically OR any combination of color mask constants to restrict the available color modes. The predefined color mask constants are:\
\fs14\
\pard \s63 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6498 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f0 \b \fs28 Mode\tab Color Mask Constant\
\fs6\
\pard \s61 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6497 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f1 \b0 \fs28 Grayscale-Alpha\tab NSColorPanelGrayModeMask\
\fi0 Red-Green-Blue\tab NSColorPanelRGBModeMask \
\fi0 Cyan-Yellow-Magenta-Black\tab NSColorPanelCMYKModeMask\
\fi0 Hue-Saturation-Brightness\tab NSColorPanelHSBModeMask\
\fi0 Custom palette\tab NSColorPanelCustomPaletteModeMask\
\fi0 Custom color list\tab NSColorPanelColorListModeMask \
\fi0 Color wheel\tab NSColorPanelWheelModeMask \
\fi0 All of the above\tab NSColorPanelAllModesMask \
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 When an application\'27s instance of NSColorPanel is masked for more than one color mode, your program can set its active mode by invoking the \b setMode:\b0  method with a color mode constant as its argument; the user can set the mode by clicking buttons on the panel. Here are the standard color modes and mode constants:\
\fs14\
\pard \s63 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6498 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f0 \b \fs28 Mode\tab Color Mode Constant\
\fs6\
\pard \s61 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6497 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f1 \b0 \fs28 Grayscale-Alpha\tab NSGrayModeColorPanel\
\fi0 Red-Green-Blue\tab NSRGBModeColorPanel \
\fi0 Cyan-Yellow-Magenta-Black\tab NSCMYKModeColorPanel\
\fi0 Hue-Saturation-Brightness\tab NSHSBModeColorPanel\
\fi0 Custom palette\tab NSCustomPaletteModeColorPanel \
\fi0 Custom color list\tab NSColorListModeColorPanel \
\fi0 Color wheel\tab NSWheelModeColorPanel \
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 In grayscale-alpha, red-green-blue, cyan-magenta-yellow-black, and hue-saturation-brightness modes, the user adjusts colors by manipulating sliders. In the custom palette mode, the user can load an NSImage file (TIFF or EPS) into the NSColorPanel, then select colors from the image. In custom color list mode, the user can create and load lists of named colors. The two custom modes provide NSPopUpLists for loading and saving files. Finally, color wheel mode provides a simplified control for selecting colors. \
\fs14\
\fs28 These constants are defined in \b AppKit/NSColorPanel.h\b0 .\
\fs14\
